# Store every text file as LF in the repo AND check it out as LF, whatever the platform or
# core.autocrlf says (it is on at the Git-for-Windows system level, which is what puts CRLF in
# a Windows worktree). text=auto leaves git's binary detection alone; eol=lf is the checkout
# half, which is the half that was missing.
#
# A policy declaration, not a conversion: every blob here is ALREADY lf (`git ls-files --eol`
# reads i/lf or i/-text for all 130 tracked files), so nothing is renormalised and no commit
# changes. What it ends is a worktree that disagrees with the repo it came from -- on Windows
# all 94 files this rule governs check out CRLF -- which stays invisible until something reads
# the bytes rather than the lines.
#
# ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING: a later line wins, so this one has to come FIRST or it would take the
# pins below back off, and those are not cosmetic (see SVR0001).
* text=auto eol=lf

# The shader sources are HASHED, and the hash is recorded in the repo
# (Shaders/spirv/sources.sha256) so the build can tell a stale bake from a current one.
# That makes their bytes part of the build contract: a working tree where core.autocrlf
# rewrote them to CRLF hashes differently from the LF blob, and SVR0001 fires on a
# checkout nobody has edited. Pin them verbatim.
#
# `-text` and not `eol=lf`: -text disables the filter outright, so the bytes on disk are
# the bytes in the object store on every platform. eol=lf still runs the filter and only
# happens to agree today. It also survives the `*` rule above, which hands every path an
# `eol` of lf: git applies eol only where `text` is set or auto-detected, and -text is
# neither, so these keep their bytes.
*.vert  -text
*.frag  -text
*.comp  -text
*.glsl  -text

# The baked output is binary; never let a filter near it.
*.spv   -text -diff

# The test font and the WebView2 loaders. Git's own detection already gets these right, and
# `text=auto` above keeps that detection — so this changes nothing today and is here for the
# day a new one lands rather than the day the detection happens to agree. A binary with no
# NUL byte early on reads as text and gets rewritten, which is a corrupted fixture and a
# checkout that still passes.
src/SdlVulkan.Renderer.Tests/Fixtures/*.ttf              -text
src/SdlVulkan.Renderer.WebView.Native/runtimes/**/*.dll  -text
